The gray backrest shifted behind the opening and settled closer to the console.

Evan stepped around Diane.

She caught his wrist.

“Maintenance route,” she said. “Now.”

He redirected his urgency toward the maintenance route. He followed her down one flight before Abby called from the landing.

“You told us to stay together.”

Diane stopped.

The booth door stood above them with the intact seal. The service route began below the stage in the dimmer room. Leaving Abby alone at the door preserved one angle and broke every other rule. Taking both students exposed the maintenance access. Diane had already shown Evan enough of the route for him to find it again.

“All three,” Diane said. “Camera continuous. Nobody moves ahead of me.”

They descended through the auditorium. The gray chair remained visible in the booth opening until the rear wall blocked their view. Onstage, the abandoned monitor still showed the booth feed. The chair occupied the center of the image, square to the camera. No person crossed behind it.

Diane stopped beside the road case.

“Time.”

“Five twenty-five and fourteen seconds,” Abby said.

“Show the monitor, then follow.”

Abby recorded the chair, the live timecode, the empty console, and the booth door. The image showed intermittent blocks across the lower corners. Automatic gain brightened the wall, reduced it, and brightened it again.

Evan leaned toward the speaker.

“Wayne?”

Diane pulled him back by the shoulder.

“No questions.”

“We are past controlled conditions.”

“We are still inside a building with doors, wiring, heights, and two students under my supervision.”

A low electrical growl came through the monitor. For a moment Diane thought she heard Evan’s name within it, but the syllables vanished when she listened directly.

Abby heard something else. “That sounded like ‘wait.’”

“It sounded like gain distortion,” Diane said.

Evan looked at Abby. “You heard a word.”

“I heard something that could become a word.”

Diane switched off the monitor speaker and left the picture running.

They crossed backstage to the dimmer room. The door carried two warning placards and a handwritten notice about obsolete circuits. Diane unlocked it with the electrical-room key. Hot air carrying metal dust came from inside.

Rows of old dimmer cabinets occupied one wall. New digital control racks stood opposite them, their fans producing a continuous dry whine. At the rear, a fixed steel ladder rose to a square ceiling panel secured with two wing bolts.

Diane pointed Abby’s lens toward the panel.

“Maintenance access to cable plenum. Panel bolts remain seated. No public access. Booth wall hatch lies approximately twenty feet north.”

Evan studied the ladder. “Anyone with this key could enter.”

“Facilities, theater technical staff, and district electricians carry authorized access.”

“So the booth retained another access route.”

“The maintenance panel remained bolted. The booth door seal remained intact. Exclusion always has defined limits.”

“Limits you mentioned after the chair appeared.”

Diane looked at him. “The crawlspace was in the setup record.”

Abby said, “It was. I filmed the access diagram.”

Evan’s attention shifted from argument to route. “The booth camera can see the interior hatch?”

“No. The hatch sits behind the equipment rack, outside its frame.”

“Then somebody could enter unseen.”

“After opening two bolts above us, crossing the plenum, and releasing a latched steel panel. Abby has continuous footage of this room from the moment we entered.”

“Not before.”

“Correct.”

Her agreement left him no reason to claim triumph. Diane had begun by insisting that incomplete coverage remained incomplete. The same principle now admitted an ordinary path into the room.

She clipped her keys inside the zippered chest pocket of her work shirt. The long boiler key pressed flat against her sternum.

“Abby stays below and records the ladder and panel. Evan stays beside her.”

Evan raised both hands. “I want a record of the crawlspace.”

“You want footage. I need space to move.”

“Take the camera.”

“The camera reduces my balance and occupies one hand.”

“Use my phone.”

“Your phone remains onstage.”

Evan glanced toward Abby’s open equipment bag. A small auxiliary camera rested in the side pocket.

“Leave that below as well,” Diane said.

Diane climbed.

The ladder transmitted every shift of her weight into the dimmer cabinets. Metal struck metal in a pattern that resembled distant footsteps. At the ceiling, she turned the first wing bolt, then the second. Abby stated each action for the recording below.

Diane pushed the panel upward.

Warm stale air folded around her face. Dust, cloth insulation, heated cable jackets, and old wood crowded the first breath until the space seemed to enter her before she entered it. She raised her flashlight into the plenum.

The space stood four feet high at its center and narrowed beneath roof trusses. Cable trays ran toward the booth. A plywood service path crossed joists darkened by decades of dust. Footprints marked the path in both directions—work boots, flat sneakers, a narrow tread from an electrician’s ladder inspection. Their edges overlapped beyond any useful chronology.

Diane pulled herself through and replaced the panel loosely behind her. Abby’s camera light shone upward around its edges.

“State your position,” Abby called.

“Inside the cable plenum. Moving north on the plywood path.”

Diane crawled.

The ceiling pressed close enough for conduit straps to rake her shoulder and catch the back of her shirt. Each knee placement flexed the plywood beneath her weight. The bend traveled through the joists, entered the dark ahead, and returned as a second impact.

Left knee.

A second impact.

Right knee.

A second impact.

She stopped.

The returning cadence stopped after one final contact.

Below, Evan whispered something to Abby.

“Quiet beneath me,” Diane called.

“We are still,” Abby answered.

Diane moved one knee forward.

A contact sounded near the booth wall.

She moved the other.

A second contact followed, accompanied by a brief jingle.

Several small keys.

One heavier strike after them.

Her own ring lay zipped against her chest. She pressed a palm over the pocket and felt no movement.

A cable tray hung to her left. Short safety chains linked its removable covers. A shift in the plywood could travel into those chains. The delayed strike could come from a loose connector against metal. The plenum narrowed direction until every sound seemed close.

Diane crawled another three feet.

The sounds continued at the same pace as her movement.

Abby called from below. “We hear the impacts.”

“Record them. Hold your positions.”

Evan’s voice followed. “The booth feed is still active.”

“You stay beneath the panel.”

A white light moved across the joists behind Diane.

She twisted around.

Evan’s face appeared above the open ceiling panel. He held Abby’s small auxiliary camera between both hands.

“Go down,” Diane said.

“We need continuity.”

“You need to obey the safety instruction.”

He lifted himself through.

The plywood shifted under their combined weight. Dust fell through the panel onto Abby’s lens.

“Evan,” Abby said from below. “Get out.”

He stayed on his knees, camera pointed at Diane. “You are in frame.”

“You have compromised the access record and increased the load on this path.”

“The room was already reachable.”

“Reachability and undocumented entry are separate facts.”

Diane considered sending him back. Turning him around in the narrow space would require both bodies to move past each other near the open panel. Leaving him behind invited another advance. The booth lay twelve feet ahead.

“Follow at six feet,” she said. “Touch nothing. Keep the camera on my back and the path.”

Evan nodded.

“State that you entered against instruction.”

His expression tightened.

“State it.”

“Evan Kruse entered the cable plenum after Diane instructed me to remain below.”

“Time,” Abby called.

“Five twenty-seven and two seconds.”

Diane continued.

The impacts changed after Evan joined her. Some matched his movement. Others arrived after both of them stopped. The cable tray gave a small metallic tremor each time the plywood bowed. A loose chain near the booth wall produced the delayed heavy tap.

Diane reached it and held the chain still.

“One identified source,” she said.

Evan filmed her hand around the links.

A key ring sounded beyond the booth hatch.

The chain in Diane’s hand remained quiet.

Evan’s camera lowered a fraction.

“Keep filming,” she said.

The booth hatch formed a rectangular seam in the block wall ahead. Two slide bolts secured it from the plenum side. Both sat in their brackets. Dust crossed the upper bolt in a smooth layer. The lower bolt held a fresh crescent where metal had rubbed metal, though routine vibration could account for it.

Diane recorded the condition aloud.

“Upper and lower bolts seated. Surface dust on upper bolt. Recent wear visible on lower track. No conclusion on timing.”

She drew the upper bolt.

Metal scraped across the bracket.

The booth feed speaker, far below onstage, reproduced a distorted scrape through the auditorium.

She drew the lower bolt and pulled the hatch toward her.

Cooler air entered the plenum.

Behind the hatch stood the rear of the booth equipment rack. A narrow gap separated it from the wall. Diane turned sideways and lowered one foot to the floor inside.

The room appeared empty beyond the rack.

“Camera stays in the plenum,” she told Evan. “Film the entry and my full body.”

He extended the lens through the hatch.

Diane lowered herself into the booth.

The camera on the tripod remained active. Its tally light shone red. The gray chair stood between tripod and glassless opening, facing the auditorium. The door seal remained visible from inside, unbroken across jamb and frame. The deadbolt lever sat horizontal in the locked position.

No person occupied the console recess, the wall behind the door, or the space beneath the counter.

Diane raised both hands for Evan’s camera.

“Booth entered through maintenance hatch at five twenty-eight. Door seal intact from interior. One chair present. Primary camera recording.”

She circled the room slowly.

The chair’s orange tape matched bay C. Its damaged glide showed the exposed crescent of steel. The booth floor between hatch and chair carried dust disturbed by old cable work, tripod placement, and Diane’s own setup inspection. Several partial shoe marks overlapped near the equipment rack. None formed a complete route.

The chair seat held a brass key.

Water coated it.

A shallow wet outline followed the key’s length across the gray plastic. No water marked the chair back, surrounding floor, ceiling, or console. The air inside the booth felt dry and warmer than the auditorium.

Diane crouched.

The key measured almost four inches. Its round bow carried no school stamp, room number, or manufacturer code. The bit used an old stepped pattern unlike the district’s current pin-tumbler keys. Dark tarnish filled the grooves. Water gathered along the shaft and fell from the tip onto the seat.

Evan moved closer to the hatch.

“What key is that?”

“I have no identification.”

“Leave it where it is.”

Diane photographed it in place with the booth camera’s still function, keeping the running video active. She placed a clean lens cloth beside the key, lifted it by the bow, and turned it beneath the tripod lens.

“Unidentified brass key recovered from chair seat. Wet surface. No visible marking. Position documented before movement.”

“Put it back,” Evan said.

“It becomes controlled evidence.”

“Your pocket is not controlled.”

“Neither is a chair that changed rooms.”

She wrapped the key in the cloth and placed it in the empty zippered pocket opposite her working keys.

She collected the key partly to prevent an immediate claim. Evan’s camera could present it as proof before anyone identified its lock, owner, or history. Diane wanted any interpretation to begin with physical examination.

Evan entered the booth through the hatch.

